                DIGITAL ANNOUNCES SECOND-GENERATION,
              HIGH-PERFORMANCE PCI-TO-PCI BRIDGE CHIPS


 ...Targeted at Desktop, Server, Adapter Card, Embedded Markets...


MAYNARD, Mass., March 11, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced a new family of high-performance, second-generation 
PCI-to-PCI bridge chips with enhancements to increase the I/O 
performance of desktop and server systems, adapter cards, and 
embedded devices.  The new 21150, 21152, and 21153 PCI-to-PCI 
bridge chips comply with the Revision 2.1 of the PCI Local Bus 
Specification, including support for delayed transactions.

     "Digital developed the first PCI-to-PCI bridge chip and has 
captured the majority of PCI bridge designs, effectively setting the 
standard for these devices," said William N. Johnson, Digital 
Semiconductor vice president of sales and marketing.  "The new chips 
are again setting the pace with support for larger numbers of PCI 
peripheral and network devices and by increasing PCI bus efficiency 
and performance."

Extending the PCI Bus

     PCI-to-PCI bridge chips enable extension of a system's I/O 
support capability by enabling motherboard designers to add more PCI 
devices and PCI option card slots than a single PCI bus can support.  
Each PCI-to-PCI bridge chip added to the motherboard creates a new 
PCI bus to support additional PCI slots or devices.  A PCI-to PCI 
bridge also enables adapter card designers to overcome the single 
load limitation of the PCI Specification by providing an independent 
PCI bus on the adapter card, to which as many as nine devices can be 
added.

Improved Performance

     Digital's new bridge family implements an enhanced buffer 
architecture that makes effective use of PCI delayed transactions, 
improving performance particularly during high I/O traffic 
conditions.  The new architecture enables the buffering of all PCI 
transactions, including memory reads, I/O and configuration 
transactions.  In addition, the buffer architecture supports 
simultaneous multiple transactions in both directions.  Separate 
posted write, read, and delayed transaction queues are provided, 
with significantly deeper buffers than first-generation PCI-to-PCI 
bridges.

New PCI-to-PCI Bridge Family Members

     The 21150 bridge chip is packaged in a 208-pin plastic quad 
flat pack (PQFP) and provides support for up to nine devices behind 
the bridge, using on-chip logic.  Ideal for embedded designs, it 
contains a JTAG interface four general-purpose I/O pins.

     The 21152 chip is pin-to-pin compatible with the 21052 bridge 
chip, which began shipping in October 1995.  Like the 21052, the 
21152 chip supports four PCI devices with on-chip logic and is 
packaged in a 160-pin PQFP. 

     The 21153 chip features a 64-bit primary interface to 
accommodate 64-bit PCI buses typically found on high-performance 
servers and workstations.  To match the wider data path, the 21153 
chip contains larger buffers than the 21150 and 21152.  It is 
packaged in a 256-point ball grid array (BGA) assembly.

     The 21150, 21152, and 21153 all incorporate the new buffer 
architecture with support for PCI delayed transactions.

     As with all chip products, Digital Semiconductor offers 
extensive design and implementation support services with the new 
bridge chips, to enable motherboard and add-in card vendors to reach 
the market quickly with new designs.

Pricing, Availability

     Prices and availability of the new PCI-to-PCI bridge chips are 
as follows:

     Chip	 Price		  Samples         Volume
         	 (5,000 units)	  Available       Shipment
     
     21150	 $23		  April		  May
     21152	 $21		  May		  June
     21153	 $39		  August	  September

Evaluation boards to assist customers with design activities will 
also be offered for each of the new bridge chips.

     Digital Semiconductor, a Digital Equipment Corporation business 
headquartered in Hudson, Massachusetts, designs, manufactures and 
markets industry-leading semiconductor products including Alpha 
microprocessors and PCI chips for networking, bridging, and multi-
media, as well as low-power StrongARM microprocessors under license 
from Advanced RISC Machines Ltd.  Mitsubishi Electric Company is a 
second source for Alpha microprocessors.  World Wide Web site:  
http://www.digital.com/info/semiconductor

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
