              DIGITAL PROVIDES $5.7 MILLION CUSTOMIZED
             COMPUTER SYSTEM FOR U.S. NAVY CASS PROJECT


     Alpha Technology Will Improve Performance & Decrease Costs

  
MAYNARD, Mass., March 14, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced the signing of a $5.7 million, two-year contract 
involving Lockheed Martin Corporation of Orlando, Florida and End To 
End Corporation of Virginia Beach, Virginia to provide a customized 
testing system for the U.S. Navy.  The system will assist the Navy 
in evolving significant performance and cost benefits into the 21st 
century through use of the Consolidated Automated Support System 
(CASS) concept.

     CASS, an advanced-technology automated test system used by the 
Navy and other government agencies, is engineered to keep pace with 
improved technologies and new requirements while assuring full 
logistic commonality and TPS transportability. Every CASS 
configuration shares the same open architecture using standard 
software, hardware, and user interfaces. This ensures asset 
interchangeability and common operating procedures across all users.

     This newest customized testing system from Digital - which 
includes a ruggedized Digital AlphaServer 1000, custom software 
drivers and firmware - will allow the U.S. Navy to migrate all 
existing application software to a new platform with no changes to 
the source code. This will result in a five-fold improvement in 
performance and 25 percent improvement in reliability at reduced cost.

     "The introduction of Digital's industry-leading 64-bit Alpha 
technology into this customized testing system provides superior CPU 
power coupled with even more reliability at a lower overall cost," 
said Gene Nelson, Digital's vice president of Computer Special 
Systems.  "We've been working with Lockheed Martin since 1986 and 
are excited to help them continue the evolution to achieve the 
highest level of CASS program requirements through our powerful 21st 
century technology."

     According to Victor A. Bloom, Lockheed Martin CASS program 
director, "This modification to CASS is via a Value Engineering 
Change Proposal (VECP), a methodology which ensures improved 
performance, state-of-the-art technology, increased reliability, and 
guaranteed cost savings.  This contract is another step in the long, 
successful involvement of Digital as part of the CASS team."  

     The customized system is used to address the Navy's 
requirements for shipboard computing - including shock and 
vibration, temperature fluctuations, and electromagnetic emissions 
and susceptibility.  It will also help to increase repair 
capabilities, decrease parts and training requirements, and reduce 
the physical space currently necessary for electronic testing.  The 
system will involve the testing of avionics and communications 
equipment, radar sets, and electro-optic devices used on board 
aircraft carriers as well as at shore sites.  

     "Digital's ability to outpace technological improvements 
coupled with coordinated teaming efforts between the three suppliers 
are key to producing quality and cost effective products for the 
CASS program," said Larry C. Delone, President and CEO of End to 
End.  "Together, we are solidifying CASS's 21st century objectives 
and delivering superior computing performance for the U.S. Navy."

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
